<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hark — INHERITED entitlements for child Mach-Os inside the app bundle
  (Electron's Helper processes AND our Swift sidecar `harkd`).

  electron-builder applies this (mac.entitlementsInherit) to every nested
  executable it signs. The key line is com.apple.security.inherit: a child
  signed with this entitlement INHERITS the parent app's entitlements and,
  crucially for us, its CODE-SIGNING IDENTITY context — so the TCC
  audio-capture grant attributes to the Hark app, not to a standalone
  "harkd" identity. This is the unlock for ADR-0011 #18 (Process Taps as a
  child process keeping the grant).

  WHY harkd needs the SAME inherited entitlements:
    * harkd opens the microphone (mic source) -> needs audio-input, inherited.
    * harkd creates a Core Audio Process Tap for system audio -> the tap is
      gated by kTCCServiceAudioCapture, which the OS prompts for (public
      path, NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription) and attributes to the Launch
      Services app. Inheriting identity is what makes that attribution be
      "Hark", which is what makes the grant STICK across launches.
    * harkd does CoreML inference (WhisperKit + diarization). CoreML / the
      ANE compiler can JIT — under hardened runtime that needs allow-jit.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      THE load-bearing line. A nested executable signed with cs.inherit
      runs under the parent's sandbox/entitlement context and identity.
      Without it, harkd would have its OWN code identity and TCC would
      attribute the audio-capture grant to "harkd" (or worse, refuse to
      remember it), breaking the persist-across-launch behavior ADR-0011
      depends on.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.inherit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      CoreML / Accelerate / the Swift runtime can generate executable code
      at runtime (ANE model compilation, Metal shader JIT). Under hardened
      runtime that is blocked without allow-jit. Narrow JIT permission,
      not the broader allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Microphone (TCC Microphone service). Inherited so harkd can open the
      mic capture source. Paired with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
